>
  <p>
    Washington City sits just east of St. George, but it has its own municipal code and its own Building Department, and its
    fence rules are not the same as St. George's. The biggest difference homeowners notice is the front yard: Washington City's
    limit near the street is 3 feet, not St. George's 4. This page summarizes the city's rules from the code itself, as read on
    September 26, 2026.
  </p>

  <h2>Washington City fence and wall rules at a glance</h2>
  <IconList items={rules} />

  <h2>How the rules play out on a typical lot</h2>
  <p>
    For most homes, the practical result is a 6-foot privacy fence or block wall along the side and rear lot lines, stepping
    down to 3 feet as it approaches the street. If you want something taller than 6 feet, the code only allows it inside the
    buildable area of the lot, and it needs a building permit. Where your lot and your neighbor's sit at different grades,
    §9-14-12 lets the fence on the shared line be built to the maximum height allowed on either side.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the project includes holding back soil with boulders, the rockery rule in §10-5-9 matters: from 3 feet tall, the wall needs
    a permit from Public Works and an engineering analysis, and rockery walls, including terraced ones combined, cannot exceed
    8 feet. For block or poured retaining walls, ask the Building Department what applies to your design; see the
    <a href="/retaining-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">retaining wall cost guide</a> for engineering and drainage costs.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>
    No Washington City fence or wall company publishes prices. As of {COST_AS_OF}, typical residential fence projects in the St.
    George area run about {fenceProject.low}–{fenceProject.high} installed (median near {fenceProject.median}), and CMU block walls about
    {blockWall.perSqFt} per square foot. The <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> breaks this down by
    material and lists every source.
  </p>

  <h2>HOAs in Washington City</h2>
  <p>
    If your home is in an HOA, its fence and wall standards apply on top of the city's. HOA approval is separate from city
    rules, and neither replaces the other. Our <a href="/hoa-fence-requirements-st-george-ut/">HOA guide</a> explains how to
    work through both.
  </p>

  <h2>Who to call</h2>
  <div class="sgfw-facts">
    <dl>
      <dt>Washington City Building Department</dt>
      <dd>111 North 100 East, Washington, UT 84780 · (435) 656-6326 · Monday to Thursday 7:30 a.m. to 5:30 p.m., Friday 8 a.m. to noon (city website, checked September 26, 2026)</dd>
      <dt>Before you dig</dt>
      <dd>Call 811 (<a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">Blue Stakes of Utah</a>) at least 3 business days ahead.</dd>
      <dt>The code</dt>
      <dd><a href={code} rel="noopener">§9-14-12, fence and wall heights</a> · <a href={rockery} rel="noopener">§10-5-9, rockery walls</a></dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
  <p class="sgfw-note">
    This page summarizes Washington City Code as published on the city's code library (shown as current through supplement
    2026 S-22 when read on September 26, 2026). It is not legal advice; the city's code and interpretation control.
  </p>
